1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,571
This video practises program understanding on five short Java programs from the question bank.

2
00:00:07,021 --> 00:00:20,375
First, four versions of linear search. We draw an object diagram for each one at the moment it finds a match. Then a small class hierarchy, where we find the target of each polymorphic call.

3
00:00:20,825 --> 00:00:24,708
The slides list what we know about a program when we understand it.

4
00:00:25,158 --> 00:00:29,510
We know its inputs and its outputs. Outputs include the errors.

5
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:34,611
We know its static structure: the data structures and the class diagram.

6
00:00:35,061 --> 00:00:43,210
We know its dynamic execution: the call graph, pointers and aliasing, and object diagrams at points in time.

7
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:48,567
And we know its algorithms: runtime, space, and when the program ends.

8
00:00:49,017 --> 00:00:59,086
The four linear search programs have the same inputs, outputs and algorithm. They differ only in static structure and in dynamic execution.

9
00:00:59,536 --> 00:01:13,808
Here are the four programs of questions-pre section 2.2. The input is a list of strings on the command line. The first string is the token to search for. The program searches for it in the rest of the list.

10
00:01:14,258 --> 00:01:20,978
The output is found it, or didn't find it. If no list is given, the program fails with an error.

11
00:01:21,428 --> 00:01:27,487
The four programs use two data structures: an array, or a linked list of Node objects.

12
00:01:27,937 --> 00:01:33,099
And they use two control structures: a loop, or a method that calls itself.

13
00:01:33,549 --> 00:01:46,477
The algorithm is the same in all four: linear search. The runtime is order n, the space is order n, and the program ends when it finds the token or reaches the end of the list.

14
00:01:46,927 --> 00:01:53,925
Each solution is an object diagram. This is the notation from the course notes, section 2.2.

15
00:01:54,375 --> 00:01:59,516
The PC tag gives the program counter: the line that is about to execute.

16
00:01:59,966 --> 00:02:07,454
A stack frame has one slot for each local variable, parameters included. A method call adds a frame.

17
00:02:07,904 --> 00:02:16,181
An object in the heap is an ellipse. Its name is the class name plus a number that you choose. Only new makes an object.

18
00:02:16,631 --> 00:02:25,762
A solid arrow is a field of one object that refers to another object. The label is the field name or the array index.

19
00:02:26,212 --> 00:02:38,564
A dotted arrow is a variable on the stack that refers to an object. A null variable has no arrow. Arrows go from the stack or the heap into the heap, never from stack to stack.

20
00:02:39,014 --> 00:02:53,179
Question 2.2.1. This is the array and loop version. The input on the command line is Moe, Larry, Curly, Moe. Draw the object diagram at the moment the program finds the match.

21
00:02:53,629 --> 00:02:55,272
Pause the video and try it.

22
00:02:55,722 --> 00:03:07,242
Before line 3 runs, the runtime made the args array. It has four slots. Slot 0 and slot 3 refer to Moe. Slot 1 is Larry, slot 2 is Curly.

23
00:03:07,692 --> 00:03:15,969
Line 3. toFind gets the object at index 0, so toFind refers to Moe. No new object is made.

24
00:03:16,419 --> 00:03:23,822
Line 4. The loop starts with i equal to 1. Line 5 compares Moe with Larry. They are not equal.

25
00:03:24,272 --> 00:03:27,664
i becomes 2. Moe is not equal to Curly.

26
00:03:28,114 --> 00:03:39,335
i becomes 3. Moe equals Moe. The program is about to run line 6 and print found it. So the PC is 6. This is the diagram for the solution.

27
00:03:39,785 --> 00:03:53,759
There is one frame, main. The slot i changes in place. The posted solution draws one Moe object for index 0 and index 3. equals compares the letters, so the result is the same.

28
00:03:54,209 --> 00:04:04,619
Question 2.2.2. The same array, but search calls itself. Same input. Draw the object diagram when the program finds the match.

29
00:04:05,069 --> 00:04:06,712
Pause the video and try it.

30
00:04:07,162 --> 00:04:15,055
Line 3 runs as before. main has two slots: args refers to the array, and toFind refers to Moe.

31
00:04:15,505 --> 00:04:33,319
Line 4 calls search with i equal to 1. A call makes a new frame with one slot per parameter: toFind, a, and i. toFind and a are copies of references, so they refer to the same objects. Line 9 compares Moe with Larry. No.

32
00:04:33,769 --> 00:04:43,539
Line 13. i is less than 3, so search calls itself with i equal to 2. A second search frame appears. Moe is not Curly.

33
00:04:43,989 --> 00:04:51,136
The third call has i equal to 3. Moe equals Moe. The next line is 10, so the PC is 10.

34
00:04:51,586 --> 00:05:03,191
The solution has four frames: main and three search frames. Each search frame has its own i. The old frames stay on the stack, because no call has returned yet.

35
00:05:03,641 --> 00:05:13,071
Compare the two array programs. In the loop version, the call graph is only main. One frame holds i, and i changes in place.

36
00:05:13,521 --> 00:05:20,539
In the recursive version, main calls search, and search calls itself. The call graph has a loop on search.

37
00:05:20,989 --> 00:05:30,099
At the match, the loop version has one frame. The recursive version has main plus one search frame per element it visited, here three.

38
00:05:30,549 --> 00:05:38,527
In the worst case the token is not there, and the recursion makes one frame per element. That is order n stack space.

39
00:05:38,977 --> 00:05:53,015
Question 2.2.3. Now the program first copies the array into a linked list of Node objects. Then it searches the list with a loop. Same input. Draw the object diagram at the match.

40
00:05:53,465 --> 00:05:55,107
Pause the video and try it.

41
00:05:55,557 --> 00:06:06,885
First, the Node class. Both fields are final, so the constructor sets them once and they never change. Line 5 sets head to null. A null variable has no arrow.

42
00:06:07,335 --> 00:06:19,879
The loop starts at the end of the array, with i equal to 3. new makes Node 1. Its next is the old head, null, and its data refers to Moe. Then head refers to Node 1.

43
00:06:20,329 --> 00:06:31,359
i equal to 2. new makes Node 2. Its next is Node 1, and its data is Curly. The old head arrow is erased. head now refers to Node 2.

44
00:06:31,809 --> 00:06:40,107
i equal to 1. Node 3 gets next equal to Node 2, and data equal to Larry. head refers to Node 3.

45
00:06:40,557 --> 00:06:56,109
i equal to 0. The loop ends. The list reads Larry, Curly, Moe, in the same order as the array. The program builds from the back because the next field is final. A node needs its next node to exist first.

46
00:06:56,559 --> 00:07:04,218
Now the search. Line 10 sets n to head, so n refers to Node 3. Its data is Larry. Not equal.

47
00:07:04,668 --> 00:07:13,841
Line 16 sets n to n dot next. The arrow from n is erased and drawn again, to Node 2. Curly. Not equal.

48
00:07:14,291 --> 00:07:18,217
n moves again, to Node 1. Its data is Moe. Equal.

49
00:07:18,667 --> 00:07:25,813
The next line is 13, so the PC is 13. There is one frame. Only the arrow from n moved.

50
00:07:26,263 --> 00:07:35,074
The slot i belongs to the first loop, which is over. The posted solution keeps an empty i slot. You can also leave it out.

51
00:07:35,524 --> 00:07:47,385
Question 2.2.4. The last variant. It builds the same linked list, then search calls itself on n dot next. Draw the object diagram at the match.

52
00:07:47,835 --> 00:07:49,478
Pause the video and try it.

53
00:07:49,928 --> 00:08:05,971
The list is the same as before. The posted solution shows i equal to 0, the last value of the loop. Line 10 calls search with head. The new frame has toFind and n. n refers to Node 3. Larry is not Moe.

54
00:08:06,421 --> 00:08:14,741
Line 22 calls search with n dot next. A second frame. Its n refers to Node 2. Curly is not Moe.

55
00:08:15,191 --> 00:08:23,660
A third frame. Its n refers to Node 1. Moe equals Moe. The next line is 19, so the PC is 19.

56
00:08:24,110 --> 00:08:34,073
The stack has main and three search frames. Each frame has its own n. Line 16, the return when n is null, does not run for this input.

57
00:08:34,523 --> 00:08:41,691
Here are the four solutions side by side. The PC at the match is 6, 10, 13 and 19.

58
00:08:42,141 --> 00:08:48,306
The recursive versions have main plus three search frames. The iterative versions have only main.

59
00:08:48,756 --> 00:08:55,391
The linked list versions add three Node objects to the heap. new runs three times, once per node.

60
00:08:55,841 --> 00:09:03,627
In each program, something different moves: the value of i, a new frame, the arrow from n, or a new frame per node.

61
00:09:04,077 --> 00:09:15,789
All four use order n space. The constant factors differ: nodes and frames add more memory. This is why the slides list both big O and constant factors.

62
00:09:16,239 --> 00:09:30,319
Program understanding includes the errors. We ran the four programs on short inputs. This check is ours, not from the posted solution. With Moe, Larry, Curly, Moe, all four print found it.

63
00:09:30,769 --> 00:09:38,428
With Moe, Larry, Curly, the token is not in the rest of the list. All four print didn't find it and exit with 1.

64
00:09:38,878 --> 00:09:57,225
With only Moe, three programs print didn't find it. Array Recursive fails. Line 4 calls search with i equal to 1 without a test. Line 9 reads a of 1, but the array has only one slot. Java throws an array index out of bounds exception.

65
00:09:57,675 --> 00:10:07,125
With no input, all four fail at line 3, because args of 0 does not exist. This is the error that the question bank lists.

66
00:10:07,575 --> 00:10:18,861
Now the second topic. Course notes section 2.1 gives the terms. B is the base class, or super class. It has a field x and a method foo.

67
00:10:19,311 --> 00:10:33,028
D extends B. D is the derived class, or sub class. D defines foo again. The definition in D overrides the one in B. In C plus plus terms, foo is a virtual method.

68
00:10:33,478 --> 00:10:37,019
Here is a call site, r dot foo. Which foo runs?

69
00:10:37,469 --> 00:10:42,461
If r refers to an object of class B, the call runs B dot foo.

70
00:10:42,911 --> 00:10:48,373
If r refers to an object of class D, the same call runs D dot foo.

71
00:10:48,823 --> 00:11:01,943
The class of the object decides the target, when the program runs. A call site with more than one possible target is polymorphic. A call site with only one target is monomorphic.

72
00:11:02,393 --> 00:11:22,595
Question 2.3.1. This is a simple version of the A S T code for the language F. Draw the class diagram, and the object diagram when the PC is 29 and i is 2. Give the input, the output, and the targets of the polymorphic call on line 28.

73
00:11:23,045 --> 00:11:24,688
Pause the video and try it.

74
00:11:25,138 --> 00:11:38,087
Start the class diagram at the top. Expr is abstract. It declares one abstract method, operator. An italic name means abstract: no object of class Expr can exist.

75
00:11:38,537 --> 00:11:53,385
BinaryExpr extends Expr. The hollow triangle means extends. It has two final fields, left and right, of type Expr. It declares the abstract method newBinaryExpr, and it defines toString.

76
00:11:53,835 --> 00:12:09,494
AndExpr and OrExpr extend BinaryExpr. Each one defines operator and newBinaryExpr. They are the only concrete classes, so every object in this program is an AndExpr or an OrExpr.

77
00:12:09,944 --> 00:12:18,029
Note one detail for later. toString is defined only in BinaryExpr. AndExpr and OrExpr inherit it.

78
00:12:18,479 --> 00:12:37,786
Now run main. Line 20. new makes AndExpr 1, and b refers to it. The declared type of b is BinaryExpr, but the object's class is AndExpr. The variable can refer to any BinaryExpr subclass object. This answers the comment on line 20.

79
00:12:38,236 --> 00:12:45,319
Line 21 makes an array with three slots. They are all null, so array 1 has no arrows yet.

80
00:12:45,769 --> 00:12:56,435
Line 23. a of 0 gets b. There is no new, so no new object. Slot 0 and b refer to the same object. They are aliases.

81
00:12:56,885 --> 00:13:01,131
Line 24. new makes OrExpr 1, in slot 1.

82
00:13:01,581 --> 00:13:20,205
Line 25 calls newBinaryExpr on b. b refers to an AndExpr, so line 12 runs, and new makes AndExpr 2 in slot 2. So the objects in the array are AndExpr, OrExpr, AndExpr. That answers the comment on line 22.

83
00:13:20,655 --> 00:13:24,964
The loop. With i equal to 0, e refers to AndExpr 1.

84
00:13:25,414 --> 00:13:31,046
With i equal to 1, the arrow from e is erased and drawn to OrExpr 1.

85
00:13:31,496 --> 00:13:45,043
With i equal to 2, e refers to AndExpr 2. The next line is 29. This is the requested diagram. The frame of main has args, b, a, i equal to 2, and e.

86
00:13:45,493 --> 00:13:58,613
First line 25, which the code calls monomorphic. b is assigned once, to AndExpr 1. So the call always runs AndExpr dot newBinaryExpr. One target.

87
00:13:59,063 --> 00:14:09,644
Line 28 calls operator on e. With i equal to 0, e refers to AndExpr 1. The target is AndExpr dot operator.

88
00:14:10,094 --> 00:14:18,051
With i equal to 1, e refers to OrExpr 1. The same line now runs OrExpr dot operator.

89
00:14:18,501 --> 00:14:29,104
With i equal to 2, e refers to AndExpr 2. The target is AndExpr dot operator again. This is the table in the posted solution.

90
00:14:29,554 --> 00:14:37,767
One call site, two targets, chosen by the class of the object. So line 28 is a polymorphic call site.

91
00:14:38,217 --> 00:14:46,729
The code asks if line 29 is mono or polymorphic. The posted solution does not answer. Here is our solution.

92
00:14:47,179 --> 00:15:00,342
toString is defined only in BinaryExpr. AndExpr and OrExpr do not override it. So for all three objects, the call runs BinaryExpr dot toString. One target.

93
00:15:00,792 --> 00:15:09,091
But inside toString, the call to operator is polymorphic. That call picks and or or, by the class of the object.

94
00:15:09,541 --> 00:15:25,263
So line 29 is monomorphic in this program. Java still compiles it as a virtual call. If a new subclass overrides toString, line 29 becomes polymorphic. We checked the targets by running the code.

95
00:15:25,713 --> 00:15:28,871
The input is none. main never reads args.

96
00:15:29,321 --> 00:15:42,334
With i equal to 0, line 28 prints and. Line 29 prints null and null. Both fields of AndExpr 1 are null, and joining null with a string gives the word null.

97
00:15:42,784 --> 00:15:46,667
With i equal to 1, the lines are or, then null or null.

98
00:15:47,117 --> 00:15:52,493
With i equal to 2, and, then null and null again. Six lines in total.

99
00:15:52,943 --> 00:15:59,961
The posted solution writes and without spaces. The method returns and with one space on each side.

100
00:16:00,411 --> 00:16:12,806
This slide is optional. It is from course notes section 5.4, later in the course. How does compiled code find the target? Each object has a header that names its class.

101
00:16:13,256 --> 00:16:23,944
In one translation, the header points to a type information block with a vtable. A vtable is an array of function pointers, one slot per method.

102
00:16:24,394 --> 00:16:38,154
A polymorphic call loads the slot for operator from the vtable of the object's class, then jumps there. OrExpr has its own vtable, with OrExpr dot operator in slot 0.

103
00:16:38,604 --> 00:16:47,031
The notes also show a second translation, a switch on a type number. You do not need this for question 2.3.1.

104
00:16:47,481 --> 00:16:58,169
To summarize. An object diagram shows the PC, the frames with one slot per variable, the objects, and the arrows. Only new makes an object.

105
00:16:58,619 --> 00:17:07,045
A loop keeps one frame and changes values in place. Recursion adds one frame per call, each with its own parameters.

106
00:17:07,495 --> 00:17:12,637
An immutable linked list is built from the back, because the next field is final.

107
00:17:13,087 --> 00:17:19,145
Outputs include errors. Test the short inputs: no arguments, and one argument.

108
00:17:19,595 --> 00:17:27,894
The declared type of a variable is not the class of the object. At a virtual call, the class of the object picks the target.

109
00:17:28,344 --> 00:17:41,592
A call site with more than one target is polymorphic. A call site with exactly one target is monomorphic. Next video: complexity, undecidability and programming paradigms.
